---
title: Recommendations
---

# Recommendations

## At a glance {% #at-a-glance %}

1. **Start small:** Convert a single manual, not the entire document library.
2. **Share one repository:** Keep documentation where the code lives.
3. **Review it like code:** Have changes to texts checked in code review.
4. **Publish automatically:** Regenerate the PDF and website with every change.
5. **Assign ownership:** Name one person responsible for each chapter.

## Tools

To get started, all you need is a lightweight markup language such as Markdoc, a typesetting system such as Typst and version control. What matters is less the tool than the habit of treating documentation as part of every change.

{% callout type="tip" title="Lessons from practice" %}
In the interviews, several teams reported that automatic PDF generation alone noticeably reduced resistance to the new way of working.
{% /callout %}

## Conclusion

Docs as code is not an end in itself. Introduced well, it makes documentation more reliable and reduces the effort of maintaining it – and finally gives readers the certainty that they are holding the current version.
